\ save-system.4 - write the *running* Forth system out as a bootable
\ image, so work already compiled into the dictionary doesn't have to
\ be recompiled on every startup.
\
\ Why: `relfsh` compiles shadow.4 + shell.4 from source every single
\ time it starts. Measured, 50 runs each: `relfsh -c true` 12.68s,
\ bare `relf kernel.img` 0.061s, `/bin/true` 0.040s. That is ~253ms
\ of compiling against ~1.2ms of actual engine startup - about 99.5%
\ of every invocation, and the reason the shell test suite takes a
\ minute. See PROGRESS.md's Iteration 40 entry.
\
\ This is deliberately NOT cross.4's SAVE-IMAGE. That one is a
\ host-side word that writes the target image the cross-compiler is
\ building. This one writes the image the current process is itself
\ running in, which is a different thing and much simpler, because
\ RelF images are already relocatable by design - that is the whole
\ point of RelF's relative addressing (see GOALS.md's load-bearing
\ architectural note).
\
\ Only three cells in a live system are absolute, and COLD is the
\ authority on which:
\
\   : COLD  START ! START @ FORTH-WORDLIST +! START @ DP +! ...
\
\ So exactly DP and FORTH-WORDLIST get START added at boot, and
\ subtracting it back out before writing is the entire "relocation"
\ this needs. BOOT (added to kernel.4 alongside this) is deliberately
\ kept as an offset at all times and never relocated in place, so it
\ needs no handling here at all.
\
\ The image is assembled in a heap copy (ALLOCATE) rather than by
\ mutating the live system: nothing is ever written to disk from a
\ system that is temporarily in a broken, half-unrelocated state, and
\ the copy can be scrubbed freely without consequence.
\
\ Scrubbing is what makes the same sources produce the same image.
\ Without it, an image carries whatever happened to be in the
\ interpreter's scratch buffers at save time - the first prebuilt
\ shell image contained the literal text
\ `S" /home/claude/relf/kernel-shell.img.tmp.476" SAVE-SYSTEM`, i.e.
\ the build machine's path and the builder's PID - plus a dozen cells
\ holding absolute addresses that differ every run. All of it is
\ re-initialized before use by COLD, WARM or QUIT, so zeroing it
\ costs nothing and removes every source of run-to-run variation. Forces DECIMAL for its own literals
\ and restores the caller's BASE, for the reason documented in
\ shadow.4's header.

BASE @  DECIMAL

VARIABLE SS-FID
VARIABLE SS-LEN
VARIABLE SS-BUF

CREATE SS-MAGIC 8 ALLOT

: SS-SET-MAGIC ( --- )
  \ The 8-byte CV8 magic the engine checks at load (CV8.md
  \ 4.1): "CV8", the call scale as a digit (0), the cell width, 'L' for
  \ the specialised opcodes, the format version, and the feature bits
  \ varcall | varslot | spec | lit64. Must agree with cv8.c's
  \ IMAGE_MAGIC, and with cross.4's SAVE-IMAGE, which writes the same
  \ header for the kernel.
  [CHAR] C SS-MAGIC     C!
  [CHAR] V SS-MAGIC 1+  C!
  [CHAR] 8 SS-MAGIC 2 + C!
  [CHAR] 0 SS-MAGIC 3 + C!      \ call scale 0
  1 CELLS  SS-MAGIC 4 + C!
  [CHAR] L SS-MAGIC 5 + C!
  5        SS-MAGIC 6 + C!
  15       SS-MAGIC 7 + C! ;

: SS-CELL, ( x --- )
  \ Append one cell to the image file. SS-MAGIC is reused as scratch:
  \ the magic itself has already been written by then.
  SS-MAGIC ! SS-MAGIC 1 CELLS SS-FID @ WRITE-FILE DROP ;

: SS-HEADER, ( --- )
  \ The header fields after the magic: the thread count and heads
  \ (START-relative, 0 for an empty thread), then the DOES> tail
  \ count, always 0 because DODOES carries its target inline.
  FORTH-WORDLIST @ DUP SS-CELL,
  1+ 1 DO
    FORTH-WORDLIST I CELLS + @ DUP IF START @ - THEN SS-CELL,
  LOOP
  0 SS-CELL, ;

: SS>COPY ( live-addr --- copy-addr )   \ same location, in the copy
  START @ - SS-BUF @ + ;

: SS-BLANK ( live-addr u --- )          \ zero a range in the copy
  SWAP SS>COPY SWAP 0 FILL ;

: SS-CELL ( live-addr --- )             \ zero one cell in the copy
  1 CELLS SS-BLANK ;

: SS-UNRELOCATE ( live-addr --- )
  \ Put a relocated cell back into its on-disk, START-relative form.
  \ COLD is the authority on which cells these are.
  SS>COPY DUP @ START @ - SWAP ! ;

: SS-UNRELOCATE-WORDLIST ( live-addr --- )
  \ FORTH-WORDLIST stopped being one cell when the dictionary was
  \ hashed: it is now [thread count][head 0..n-1], and COLD relocates
  \ every head. So this is SS-UNRELOCATE applied to each of them.
  \
  \ Two things here are load-bearing, and both are silent if missed.
  \ Cell 0 is the thread COUNT, not an address, and must not be
  \ touched. And an EMPTY thread is 0 and must stay 0 - subtracting
  \ START would leave -START on disk, which COLD's own ?DUP guard
  \ would then happily relocate into a pointer to the image base.
  \
  \ Getting this wrong does not produce a diagnostic. It produces an
  \ image that boots with 32 stale absolute addresses in its word
  \ list and segfaults in FIND, which is exactly what the first
  \ attempt at this did.
  DUP @ 1+ 1 DO
    DUP I CELLS + SS>COPY
    DUP @ ?DUP IF START @ - SWAP ! ELSE DROP THEN
  LOOP DROP ;

: SS-SCRUB ( --- )
  \ Everything here is scratch that COLD, WARM or QUIT re-initializes
  \ before anything reads it, and every one of them either holds an
  \ absolute address (so it differs every run) or holds leftover text
  \ from whatever the builder was doing.
  TIB             1 CELLS 80 +  SS-BLANK   \ terminal input buffer
  NAMEBUF         1 CELLS 28 +  SS-BLANK   \ FIND's name scratch
  POCKET          1 CELLS 252 + SS-BLANK   \ interpreted S" strings
  INCLUDE-BUFFER  1 CELLS 508 + SS-BLANK   \ lines of the included file
  CONTEXT         1 CELLS 28 +  SS-BLANK   \ search order (WARM resets)
  INCLUDE-POINTER SS-CELL
  SRC   SS-CELL   #SRC SS-CELL   >IN SS-CELL   SID SS-CELL
  \ #TIB is the LENGTH that goes with TIB, blanked above. Blanking the
  \ buffer but leaving its length meant the image still recorded how
  \ long the builder's last command line was - one byte of difference
  \ between `relfsh -c true` and a full test run, which is all it takes
  \ to make a tracked build artifact churn.
  \
  \ SPAN was scrubbed here too until EXPECT was retired; it existed
  \ only to hold EXPECT's result and nothing writes it now.
  #TIB  SS-CELL
  LAST  SS-CELL   CURRENT SS-CELL
  CSP   SS-CELL   'LEAVE  SS-CELL
  \ The compiler's peephole state (kernel.4's NO-PEEP). LAST-CALLXT is
  \ an ABSOLUTE address that NO-PEEP never clears, so without this the
  \ image differed on every run under ASLR (Iteration 243).
  LAST-OP SS-CELL   LAST-LIT SS-CELL   LAST-LITV SS-CELL
  LAST-CALL SS-CELL LAST-CALLXT SS-CELL
  \ HEADER's scratch: HDR-HEAD is the absolute address of a thread head.
  HDR-HEAD SS-CELL  HDR-LEN SS-CELL
  S0    SS-CELL   R0      SS-CELL
  START SS-CELL
  \ shadow.4's save-stack pointer. It is zero at save time when the
  \ frames are balanced, but scrubbing it costs nothing and makes that
  \ a property of the image rather than a hope.
  \
  \ Iteration 137's LENTER/LEXIT added six more scratch cells here
  \ (LE-A holding a code address, plus its counts) and Iteration 148
  \ scrubbed them; Iteration 149 reverted 137, so they are gone again.
  \ If that change is ever re-applied, they must come back - a rebuilt
  \ image stops reproducing otherwise, which is what found them.
  \
  \ This file therefore REQUIRES shadow.4 to be loaded first, which
  \ relfsh already does.
  LSAVE-SP SS-CELL
  \ This word's own working variables, and pool.4's - they hold a heap
  \ address and a file descriptor from the save that is in progress,
  \ so leaving them would make two saves of the same system differ.
  \ BUF-LIST is deliberately NOT scrubbed: it is the buffer
  \ declaration chain, real state the image needs.
  SS-FID SS-CELL   SS-LEN SS-CELL   SS-BUF SS-CELL
  BUF-BODY SS-CELL BUF-SIZE SS-CELL BUF-PTR SS-CELL ;

: SAVE-SYSTEM ( c-addr u --- )
  \ Writes the whole live dictionary, START through HERE, prefixed by
  \ the magic header. The result is a complete image: run it with
  \ `relf that-image` and every word defined at save time is there.
  \
  \ Buffer contents declared via pool.4's BUFFER: are NOT saved -
  \ RESET-BUFFERS releases them and marks them unallocated, so the
  \ image is written in a clean, freshly-booted state. That is what
  \ SAVE-SYSTEM is for; it is not a checkpoint/restore facility.
  RESET-BUFFERS
  HERE START @ - SS-LEN !
  SS-LEN @ ALLOCATE ABORT" SAVE-SYSTEM: out of memory" SS-BUF !
  START @ SS-BUF @ SS-LEN @ MOVE
  DP SS-UNRELOCATE
  FORTH-WORDLIST SS-UNRELOCATE-WORDLIST
  \ ...and every other wordlist, down the chain WORDLIST built. The
  \ link cells hold OFFSETS and so need no unrelocating themselves;
  \ only the thread heads inside each wordlist do. Reading the chain
  \ from the LIVE system, writing through SS>COPY, exactly as the
  \ single-wordlist case does.
  WORDLISTS @
  BEGIN ?DUP WHILE
    START @ + DUP SS-UNRELOCATE-WORDLIST
    1 CELLS - @
  REPEAT
  SS-SCRUB
  SS-SET-MAGIC
  W/O BIN CREATE-FILE ABORT" SAVE-SYSTEM: cannot create file" SS-FID !
  SS-MAGIC 8 SS-FID @ WRITE-FILE DROP
  SS-HEADER,
  SS-BUF @ SS-LEN @ SS-FID @ WRITE-FILE DROP
  SS-FID @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
  SS-BUF @ FREE DROP ;

: SET-BOOT ( xt --- )
  \ Arrange for the saved image to run xt at startup instead of
  \ printing the banner and entering the interpreter loop. Stored as
  \ an offset from START, never as an absolute address, so it stays
  \ valid across the save. COLD adds START back - see kernel.4.
  START @ - BOOT ! ;

BASE !
